 2. Lisa Cook to skip White House tribunal 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Fed governor LIsa Cook. Photo: David Paul Morris/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Lawyers for Fed governor Lisa Cook said she will not participate in a White House hearing over her attempted firing set for Dec. 14, arguing it is an unfair process with a foregone conclusion.

 Driving the news: Cook's lawyers say that the White House process is still stacked against her — "a façade designed to obscure a predetermined decision to fire Governor Cook," writes her attorney, Abbe David Lowell.

 After the Supreme Court ruled Cook can remain in place while allegations of mortgage fraud are investigated, the White House established a process to try to dismiss her with legal justification. What they're saying: "Governor Cook has no legal obligation to participate in an unfair hearing—and indeed, has a duty to the Federal Reserve to decline to participate in any proceeding that would undermine the institution's unique status and independence," writes Lowell. 

 The White House has shown it is "uninterested in convening the 'substantive and fair hearing' you have claimed, Lowell adds. "Instead, you seek only to manufacture 'cause' for a predetermined removal outcome." Zoom in: In particular, he notes that White House economic adviser Kevin Hassett is on the three-person committee organized by the White House, and has repeatedly said publicly that Cook should step down.

 He also says that the White House has requested Cook produce evidence supporting her case while not giving her lawyers equivalent access to evidence they say they have already gathered. The other side: The White House did not immediately respond to a request for comment.

 Reality check: Even if President Trump succeeds in his efforts to fire Cook, he will be hard-pressed to find a replacement candidate who both satisfies his desire for lower interest rates and is acceptable to the Senate Democrats who take control of the body in January.

 That raises the prospect that any vacancies that arise on the powerful Board of Governors could remain vacant for quite some time. 
 
